  'Introduction to Comparative Politics',
  'Compare political systems through field scope, the comparative method, concepts, measurement, and causal puzzles. Topics cover the state and state-building, nations and citizenship, democracy and democratic quality, authoritarian and hybrid regimes, constitutions and courts, parliamentary and presidential systems, electoral systems and parties, political culture and media, development and welfare states, political inclusion, protest and regime change, conflict and post-conflict governance, comparative data and research design, and a regional case portfolio.',
  $cpoljson${"slug": "introduction-to-comparative-politics", "title": "Introduction to Comparative Politics", "description": "Compare political systems through field scope, the comparative method, concepts, measurement, and causal puzzles. Topics cover the state and state-building, nations and citizenship, democracy and democratic quality, authoritarian and hybrid regimes, constitutions and courts, parliamentary and presidential systems, electoral systems and parties, political culture and media, development and welfare states, political inclusion, protest and regime change, conflict and post-conflict governance, comparative data and research design, and a regional case portfolio.", "topics": [{"id": "30a191d5-2b42-4160-a256-15bd059da8e9", "type": "topic", "title": "Comparative Politics as a Field and Method", "description": "Students learn why comparing political systems reveals patterns that single-country study can miss, while also learning the limits of comparison.", "children": [{"id": "dd535d52-db9a-4fdb-9766-0516886f4d21", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Field purpose", "children": [{"id": "68a85bd1-b924-4c42-ba78-cea7e3659309", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative politics versus American politics, IR, and political theory"}, {"id": "463e90ec-6164-4978-9538-3a8219f89e51", "type": "concept", "title": "Regimes, institutions, political behavior, and political economy"}, {"id": "f821bf60-f211-441e-8e6b-39f6f8292f2d", "type": "concept", "title": "Description, explanation, prediction, and normative evaluation"}], "description": "The point is to understand political variation across countries and explain why institutions, identities, and outcomes differ."}, {"id": "6c0089be-c52b-4c31-bf65-fb66e053f63d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative method", "children": [{"id": "095a6665-96c3-40a4-a577-1c28a8576025", "type": "concept", "title": "Most-similar and most-different case comparisons"}, {"id": "2b40e48b-6b0c-46f6-8090-a619a2d98c30", "type": "concept", "title": "Within-case and cross-national comparison"}, {"id": "67eaf761-70f9-447e-9ff6-d7726dbc42f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Selection bias and scope conditions"}], "description": "Students learn that comparison is a disciplined way of reasoning, not just lining countries up side by side."}, {"id": "c2be65a5-c83e-4049-a120-cce77fb82b33", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Concepts and measurement", "children": [{"id": "47570408-bca1-4625-9d7d-5f25b8211764", "type": "concept", "title": "Democracy, state capacity, development, identity, and violence"}, {"id": "2edebf45-3bdd-4cf0-8a3b-ff428466c0aa", "type": "concept", "title": "Operationalization and indicators"}, {"id": "5ec3f381-2867-4ead-ace2-c7160a8e6d44", "type": "concept", "title": "Validity, reliability, and contested concepts"}], "description": "Good comparison depends on defining concepts clearly and measuring them carefully."}]}, {"id": "725db581-1bd2-4761-aa7c-1c93efef9f1a", "type": "topic", "title": "The State, Sovereignty, Capacity, and State-Building", "description": "This module examines how states form, claim authority, build institutions, and deliver order, rights, and services.", "children": [{"id": "abc6ba38-1b9b-49d0-852a-58d3ead238ab", "type": "subtopic", "title": "State foundations", "children": [{"id": "3b49eafd-b3cd-4487-92b2-39addf182da6", "type": "concept", "title": "Territory, population, authority, and monopoly on legitimate violence"}, {"id": "92048feb-84d0-4ed4-9c6c-5e21e2dc2667", "type": "concept", "title": "Sovereignty and recognition"}, {"id": "758e3dac-7eaa-4e05-9621-6aad28f3de34", "type": "concept", "title": "State, regime, government, and nation distinctions"}], "description": "Students learn what makes a state distinct from a government, regime, nation, or society."}, {"id": "1e69214d-0760-417a-b03f-ca5d6cb98e55", "type": "subtopic", "title": "State-building", "children": [{"id": "5c790de8-539e-4d2c-90d0-236146a4fcb7", "type": "concept", "title": "War-making and extraction"}, {"id": "14448170-6633-4315-b3db-d7e539e651dd", "type": "concept", "title": "Bureaucracy and administrative capacity"}, {"id": "9cbde4b3-e111-4c91-9ec2-dc42697bc798", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial legacies and postcolonial state formation"}], "description": "State-building explains why some governments can tax, police, educate, and regulate while others struggle."}, {"id": "3796f552-a08d-4096-a795-5d86cd9e7882", "type": "subtopic", "title": "State capacity", "children": [{"id": "5411ea6f-78c0-41b0-aec5-d74e8e558517", "type": "concept", "title": "Taxation, public goods, rule implementation, and service delivery"}, {"id": "1791f8ba-9f5f-49c6-826a-476cdd6a9264", "type": "concept", "title": "Corruption, informality, and clientelism"}, {"id": "b9fc11e3-9d46-47d0-ab5d-07d04b146091", "type": "concept", "title": "Fragile states and governance gaps"}], "description": "Capacity links institutions to everyday outcomes people can feel."}]}, {"id": "4a2d2b96-6ea7-4e6b-96c6-b46ce30ff507", "type": "topic", "title": "Nations, Nationalism, Identity, and Citizenship", "description": "Students study how political communities are imagined, contested, included, and excluded through identity and citizenship.", "children": [{"id": "b3615fd8-253d-440e-bbf7-652eb9935b6c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Nation and nationalism", "children": [{"id": "51ae156d-d5e4-4828-b39c-94b8366391b0", "type": "concept", "title": "Civic and ethnic nationalism"}, {"id": "f9253b2d-c078-41c2-b5df-8c58322451b7", "type": "concept", "title": "Imagined communities and national myths"}, {"id": "28e182c8-4a7e-4277-81c9-03a388bf95e8", "type": "concept", "title": "National identity, borders, and belonging"}], "description": "Students learn how national identities become politically powerful and sometimes dangerous."}, {"id": "1fbf2b75-7005-4e65-ac8f-8d6f498e903a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Citizenship", "children": [{"id": "92a5f3f7-6ed4-4533-ade9-b2066c59197d", "type": "concept", "title": "Birthright, naturalization, and exclusion"}, {"id": "38dd5ba9-6384-46b4-b9c9-b253ffc868d4", "type": "concept", "title": "Migration, refugees, and statelessness"}, {"id": "2b6c6e73-7bab-44a9-99b4-e8902a4f79e6", "type": "concept", "title": "Citizenship rights and political participation"}], "description": "Citizenship defines who has rights, voice, obligations, and protection."}, {"id": "92ed6e3b-e64b-4790-be15-362b9ccf856a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Identity politics", "children": [{"id": "f2c73a38-ee89-418f-b38f-29438d32a495", "type": "concept", "title": "Language, ethnicity, religion, race, class, gender, and region"}, {"id": "6e2737d5-e41a-45a2-9b80-567415d67f8f", "type": "concept", "title": "Cross-cutting versus reinforcing cleavages"}, {"id": "ba6e7ab1-5fad-484f-939f-3fe08b01b271", "type": "concept", "title": "Identity entrepreneurs and political mobilization"}], "description": "Identity can mobilize solidarity, resistance, exclusion, and conflict."}]}, {"id": "1df364b7-24a0-4995-8d72-d31e3bd153ef", "type": "topic", "title": "Democracy, Representation, and Democratic Quality", "description": "This topic introduces democratic institutions, participation, rights, accountability, and the many ways democracies vary.", "children": [{"id": "f2bce05e-461b-4a75-8b54-f0df7a747090", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Democratic principles", "children": [{"id": "1f72d2f1-c19c-4bf2-947d-9484670a5773", "type": "concept", "title": "Competition and participation"}, {"id": "b1da4a4c-5c00-42e4-98d6-b0ae5fa8ad66", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil liberties and political rights"}, {"id": "797d231c-6129-4022-8895-886d94ff5788", "type": "concept", "title": "Rule of law, accountability, and consent"}], "description": "Students learn democracy as more than elections: it includes rights, accountability, and meaningful participation."}, {"id": "11804223-3477-4aa5-95a2-27a046639365", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Models of democracy", "children": [{"id": "71cf0865-c2d6-4f7a-9d45-ef24f87de4c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Majoritarian and consensus democracy"}, {"id": "2bdbeec0-1bab-4272-b8da-63ce40e4c861", "type": "concept", "title": "Direct, representative, deliberative, and participatory democracy"}, {"id": "ca53c309-1955-423f-9573-b3e212ad76bb", "type": "concept", "title": "Liberal, electoral, and social democracy"}], "description": "Different democracies distribute power and represent citizens in different ways."}, {"id": "3b6fd9c0-6c49-4b41-8b91-688710df14b6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Democratic quality", "children": [{"id": "4d35b0b9-288c-4e07-a5b6-c5fa7dc499e1", "type": "concept", "title": "V-Dem and Freedom House measures"}, {"id": "3e7311c2-6f97-4ad3-8a2a-2935cf9337ef", "type": "concept", "title": "Turnout, representation, inequality, corruption, and trust"}, {"id": "cc4cde12-241b-4571-91d0-152469b213d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Responsiveness and accountability"}], "description": "Students learn to evaluate democracies comparatively using evidence."}]}, {"id": "8d15284b-49a1-48d6-8da3-e7039d377a0a", "type": "topic", "title": "Authoritarian Regimes, Hybrid Regimes, and Democratic Backsliding", "description": "Students learn how nondemocratic rule works, why hybrid regimes persist, and how democratic erosion unfolds.", "children": [{"id": "92d675d2-360d-41bd-8d8e-798367b84f8a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Authoritarian types", "children": [{"id": "b345b953-5100-4384-89dd-6fee65e49de3", "type": "concept", "title": "Military, party, personalist, monarchic, and theocratic rule"}, {"id": "0fb15cf6-4f31-4d14-9124-56f065a0cb80", "type": "concept", "title": "Totalitarianism versus authoritarianism"}, {"id": "880fdb71-4296-4ab6-90e4-6a90acd803a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Selectorates, elites, coercion, and patronage"}], "description": "Students compare different nondemocratic systems rather than treating them as one category."}, {"id": "f060cb02-990d-45f8-be5f-9d735df84b45", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Hybrid regimes", "children": [{"id": "e37d6d0e-1000-4c0a-8031-1eb108e75f3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Competitive authoritarianism"}, {"id": "99ca8470-a120-4b4c-8311-eba4a0306f30", "type": "concept", "title": "Managed elections and controlled media"}, {"id": "298e247e-0379-4fcf-8896-073fe5ea6924", "type": "concept", "title": "Opposition constraints and institutional manipulation"}], "description": "Hybrid regimes use democratic-looking institutions without full democratic accountability."}, {"id": "0f5dc224-86e2-47ea-928e-d63e08b40966", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Backsliding", "children": [{"id": "d6e76586-f43b-49d7-9f91-bfbf3b62906d", "type": "concept", "title": "Court capture, media pressure, and electoral rule changes"}, {"id": "13c21b39-ee17-47fb-a5fd-977c9b5ef1d9", "type": "concept", "title": "Polarization and executive aggrandizement"}, {"id": "bcd0aaa4-a5dd-4d72-8a0e-e906e2aeabdd", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil society resistance and international pressure"}], "description": "Students learn how democracies can erode gradually through legal and institutional channels."}]}, {"id": "d18bb7ae-e18e-48ad-bfc7-1d70c8273255", "type": "topic", "title": "Constitutions, Executives, Legislatures, and Courts", "description": "This module compares institutional designs and how they structure power, bargaining, accountability, and rights.", "children": [{"id": "f3db2bc7-ce2d-4214-aa31-5d90a842095b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Constitutions", "children": [{"id": "2fbf8e7a-fc37-4b3c-8247-8f816ded9ccb", "type": "concept", "title": "Written and unwritten constitutions"}, {"id": "e5abda3e-20a4-4ea5-991b-3381a09e904a", "type": "concept", "title": "Rights catalogs and amendment rules"}, {"id": "965c1a28-7213-4000-99e6-0101d3261e11", "type": "concept", "title": "Constitutional endurance and enforcement"}], "description": "Constitutions set the formal rules for authority, rights, and institutional conflict."}, {"id": "8f07048c-89da-4389-b14d-9d5a8f48f177", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Executives and legislatures", "children": [{"id": "ac020762-8c39-45d7-bb9f-4103ae1f92cc", "type": "concept", "title": "Presidents, prime ministers, cabinets, and heads of state"}, {"id": "cf0a2a02-47ea-4665-881e-5363e3c4e0c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Unicameral and bicameral legislatures"}, {"id": "2872d98b-25fe-4cc7-819f-123265a2ca31", "type": "concept", "title": "Agenda control, confidence votes, and oversight"}], "description": "Students compare how systems organize lawmaking, oversight, and political bargaining."}, {"id": "dcd5a853-2cc3-4dac-981a-9457accbc9c2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Courts", "children": [{"id": "77cad11c-34b7-4337-98c7-1bed27e65a64", "type": "concept", "title": "Judicial review"}, {"id": "67dab922-ae58-45ea-98d1-ff87dc3779ee", "type": "concept", "title": "Judicial independence"}, {"id": "cd60d44b-8f6a-45d6-85bc-1391c34445c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Constitutional courts and legal mobilization"}], "description": "Courts can protect rights, constrain power, or become political battlegrounds."}]}, {"id": "4b3feab3-6384-41e1-b3f3-1ec2115619fe", "type": "topic", "title": "Parliamentary, Presidential, Federal, and Unitary Systems", "description": "Students compare major constitutional architectures and their consequences for governance and representation.", "children": [{"id": "ca979671-6dcb-4846-afd5-90baa3da7945", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Parliamentary and presidential systems", "children": [{"id": "aaf7dae1-325f-4532-816c-fdaad1d3f4d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Cabinet responsibility and confidence"}, {"id": "99975c36-e158-4aeb-aa88-9b869caebcf2", "type": "concept", "title": "Separation of powers"}, {"id": "dce90f6a-6ec0-47ec-905f-d618f71d63ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Coalition governments and divided government"}], "description": "Students learn how executive-legislative design shapes accountability and instability."}, {"id": "7afa9191-d9dd-4de5-bc23-8c3df1185614", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Federalism and decentralization", "children": [{"id": "aa9ec1b7-a0de-4af9-8a79-50fefc186fb0", "type": "concept", "title": "Federal, unitary, and devolved systems"}, {"id": "834c0b5a-3c1c-4998-9151-53cfd5ecb914", "type": "concept", "title": "Fiscal federalism"}, {"id": "85cb0087-a593-4adc-acc0-5b5bcdad23d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Subnational autonomy and minority accommodation"}], "description": "Territorial design affects identity, public goods, conflict, and policy variation."}, {"id": "24677b03-0afb-493f-bfbd-cee6722fdf7a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Institutional tradeoffs", "children": [{"id": "63da0bd5-ea03-4860-8877-43c8e5431e16", "type": "concept", "title": "Accountability versus inclusion"}, {"id": "7edcefa1-c114-4db1-b7bf-03d4f4c1343a", "type": "concept", "title": "Coordination versus veto points"}, {"id": "f8ea1060-8675-4bf7-98ba-9471317d8d25", "type": "concept", "title": "Stability versus responsiveness"}], "description": "No design is automatically best; institutions solve some problems and create others."}]}, {"id": "dd399bf5-9b07-496c-a9bb-a563099df05a", "type": "topic", "title": "Electoral Systems, Parties, Coalitions, and Voters", "description": "This topic connects electoral rules to party systems, campaigning, coalition formation, and voter behavior.", "children": [{"id": "93fbbe21-397e-46d0-bc31-d4b32c14ed9d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Electoral systems", "children": [{"id": "a741df34-71d7-43b4-83e4-6cb618a2cac2", "type": "concept", "title": "Single-member plurality, runoff, proportional representation, and mixed systems"}, {"id": "c6e060d1-a51f-4b64-bfbd-f632993d9df2", "type": "concept", "title": "District magnitude and thresholds"}, {"id": "85756872-0eea-49ab-9ac5-5b5f377cd650", "type": "concept", "title": "Duverger's law and mechanical/psychological effects"}], "description": "Rules translate votes into seats and shape strategic behavior."}, {"id": "6b098c11-19af-476f-b88a-1b18c479a3c6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Parties and party systems", "children": [{"id": "4317b837-3cb1-44af-91f9-2848f3f79c34", "type": "concept", "title": "Party institutionalization"}, {"id": "a47e6c69-8f5a-4e96-931e-8a0bb55eca1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Programmatic versus clientelist parties"}, {"id": "d6cbfc16-de6e-4055-8047-5ebe76da57d2", "type": "concept", "title": "Dominant-party, two-party, and multiparty systems"}], "description": "Parties organize representation, competition, policy, and governance."}, {"id": "3da67146-e35b-42c6-b777-63db27018897", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Voters and coalitions", "children": [{"id": "4fff1f05-7a34-4a9a-b187-085eab425b4b", "type": "concept", "title": "Cleavage voting, class voting, ethnic voting, and issue voting"}, {"id": "a1f2f2f2-282a-4e13-b248-6b7fdca24e31", "type": "concept", "title": "Campaigns, media, and turnout"}, {"id": "cbb41016-4970-478d-b0ca-fd4c9706fbcd", "type": "concept", "title": "Coalition bargaining and government formation"}], "description": "Students connect institutions to voter behavior and governing alliances."}]}, {"id": "4d3d5d2f-029e-4d2e-a54e-861ff13ff8b4", "type": "topic", "title": "Political Culture, Civil Society, Media, and Public Opinion", "description": "Students examine the social foundations of politics, including trust, participation, information, and civic organization.", "children": [{"id": "a51e4959-2909-4d6e-aca4-965bbac99e27", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Political culture", "children": [{"id": "e9972b37-3174-4237-86d8-bb62ccc8c2bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Trust, legitimacy, tolerance, and civic norms"}, {"id": "2a1ee3fc-c2a7-46f0-9a00-43aca1d2810c", "type": "concept", "title": "Political socialization"}, {"id": "bfce5dd7-ed43-444e-89d7-5199a123c598", "type": "concept", "title": "Culture as explanation and its limits"}], "description": "Political beliefs and norms influence how institutions are understood and used."}, {"id": "85897c1d-e245-4e43-a0ec-156731521ec7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Civil society", "children": [{"id": "1962de9d-49a2-4d1e-a513-9912caa05aee", "type": "concept", "title": "NGOs, unions, religious groups, professional groups, and social networks"}, {"id": "e82d72cd-1451-4b54-bb32-eeaf5e956470", "type": "concept", "title": "Social capital"}, {"id": "e67c8e30-6d3d-4c37-8bcd-a75a9ce85330", "type": "concept", "title": "State-civil society relations"}], "description": "Associations can check power, build trust, and mobilize citizens."}, {"id": "3fcf6fa2-1002-4785-8f8e-4fe4f042327a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Media and public opinion", "children": [{"id": "d70de851-0417-4904-b6b7-8b79c0c325c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Public opinion measurement"}, {"id": "c612b2da-d6d0-47fb-8f39-e75b45848b2c", "type": "concept", "title": "Media freedom and censorship"}, {"id": "9e6fd367-f743-4750-9c92-22a6cba04571", "type": "concept", "title": "Misinformation, propaganda, and platform politics"}], "description": "Information systems shape accountability, polarization, and political knowledge."}]}, {"id": "3b540eb3-441e-40ec-91f9-517f4c31ff0a", "type": "topic", "title": "Political Economy of Development, Inequality, and Welfare States", "description": "This module explains how institutions, markets, class, inequality, and policy shape development and redistribution.", "children": [{"id": "6368c700-cfb9-48cd-bbb3-4bda00951e74", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Development", "children": [{"id": "b1fe1eb3-cac0-4b2e-8682-e886bacc3a94", "type": "concept", "title": "Inclusive and extractive institutions"}, {"id": "98f8dda3-e307-42bf-a38c-38f8c88c5f81", "type": "concept", "title": "Modernization theory and dependency perspectives"}, {"id": "eeb8e439-9b30-438e-abf2-321dd673ca35", "type": "concept", "title": "Human development, poverty, and governance indicators"}], "description": "Students learn how political institutions shape economic growth and human development."}, {"id": "2035d47e-80d0-434f-b1f0-8ac7993be52b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Inequality and redistribution", "children": [{"id": "602c0a50-dcea-4380-b6ae-41c1caf23c93", "type": "concept", "title": "Class coalitions and labor politics"}, {"id": "cb7fcada-1147-44b6-84e0-deee3ca27e64", "type": "concept", "title": "Taxation, social insurance, and redistribution"}, {"id": "ad091aac-866b-4f3b-8325-0743fe076d29", "type": "concept", "title": "Inequality, mobility, and political voice"}], "description": "Politics determines who benefits from growth and who bears costs."}, {"id": "beb8213b-1333-489d-b36a-b7a1403dba44", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Welfare states and capitalism", "children": [{"id": "31fa6a86-04fe-43f5-aaf0-5daebd3f6832", "type": "concept", "title": "Liberal, conservative, social democratic, and developmental models"}, {"id": "c30b42ae-065a-454f-a14a-7d2b1935a243", "type": "concept", "title": "Varieties of capitalism"}, {"id": "1ff2c431-5a36-4a7a-a67a-23c3db946aae", "type": "concept", "title": "Austerity, globalization, and welfare reform"}], "description": "Countries organize markets and social protection in different ways."}]}, {"id": "203bde21-e281-40fb-9222-fefa1ed4e5d5", "type": "topic", "title": "Ethnicity, Religion, Gender, Race, and Political Inclusion", "description": "Students study how social cleavages become politically organized and how institutions can reduce or intensify exclusion.", "children": [{"id": "21fbd0a1-66a6-43a5-9af0-0483f9a56976", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Social cleavages", "children": [{"id": "e81543ee-e8ff-4b9b-892d-b1a11bebe883", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethnic, religious, racial, caste, class, gender, and regional cleavages"}, {"id": "2de12143-3b48-40af-8d9a-82de67d26507", "type": "concept", "title": "Cross-cutting and cumulative divisions"}, {"id": "f3e0f3ab-d832-4d0a-90ef-ca446b7006ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Cleavage freezing and transformation"}], "description": "Cleavages become politically important when identities align with organization, interests, and institutions."}, {"id": "4dd92414-6c27-446e-b949-6bdd3d3d485c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Inclusion and exclusion", "children": [{"id": "445689a9-9fe3-47a8-8cbb-34fb307c4055", "type": "concept", "title": "Minority rights and representation"}, {"id": "6bc48550-7ddd-44e2-b813-b20e17543a84", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender quotas and descriptive representation"}, {"id": "ee1519e9-2702-47b9-bf48-53e7ba8a7471", "type": "concept", "title": "Discrimination, segregation, and unequal citizenship"}], "description": "Political systems vary in how they include marginalized groups."}, {"id": "64478886-747b-4860-b08c-8eb50a44955e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Institutional management", "children": [{"id": "7fc6db70-be1f-4296-8003-e1dc8d9fb94f", "type": "concept", "title": "Consociationalism and power sharing"}, {"id": "a1a75086-a43c-4e0c-81ac-143acea1ef64", "type": "concept", "title": "Federalism and autonomy"}, {"id": "8c187d9b-33e7-46bb-886c-338c7fe82b0e", "type": "concept", "title": "Electoral incentives and identity moderation"}], "description": "Institutions can either lower conflict or harden divisions."}]}, {"id": "217735f9-5907-4dd6-8390-777f28db09e3", "type": "topic", "title": "Revolutions, Social Movements, Protest, and Regime Change", "description": "This topic covers collective action, contentious politics, revolutionary moments, repression, and transitions.", "children": [{"id": "1c36cd40-20f1-4510-9cd9-76880948f514", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Collective action", "children": [{"id": "1f16738c-46e2-42b4-8506-196b75e97df7", "type": "concept", "title": "Grievances, resources, and political opportunities"}, {"id": "f85edb1b-1e87-4bd2-b18a-462dfe933edd", "type": "concept", "title": "Networks, leadership, and framing"}, {"id": "62eba28d-2c5b-407f-bc39-3cec24e6cf23", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital mobilization and repression"}], "description": "Students learn why people mobilize despite risk and coordination problems."}, {"id": "d639e7ee-ac40-4475-b713-0bd234d7c40f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Revolutions and transitions", "children": [{"id": "60616ac1-eadc-465e-9fea-e7af87062c98", "type": "concept", "title": "Social revolutions"}, {"id": "04026b76-e5fa-456a-935d-7250970449c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Negotiated transitions"}, {"id": "e0e69d63-969d-4d7f-ad30-9019dd798655", "type": "concept", "title": "Coups and pacts"}], "description": "Regime change is shaped by elite splits, mass mobilization, and institutional breakdown."}, {"id": "9bb150ba-b926-486e-9a9e-2cba940b6105", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Protest outcomes", "children": [{"id": "a39ffcd0-827f-4f83-ac5f-e9d8b9b0e016", "type": "concept", "title": "Nonviolent versus violent resistance"}, {"id": "9e7c4a57-a287-4013-ba14-833c4a716d45", "type": "concept", "title": "Repression and backlash"}, {"id": "6126f997-4fbe-4ef3-9ac9-0f272d1f8576", "type": "concept", "title": "Movement institutionalization and policy change"}], "description": "Movements can democratize, radicalize, be absorbed, or be repressed."}]}, {"id": "44d06b12-5789-4918-946c-b7b4fdaf151f", "type": "topic", "title": "Conflict, Violence, Nationalism, and Post-Conflict Governance", "description": "Students analyze civil conflict, state violence, nationalism, peacebuilding, and post-conflict institutions.", "children": [{"id": "f11e3c6e-8df8-47bd-89aa-2a19417a4936", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Political violence", "children": [{"id": "5ccda3ad-2775-438c-88d2-4c5a721b9ab0", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil war, terrorism, repression, riots, and insurgency"}, {"id": "cf862d56-2ef3-4aa1-aded-ac72a4203d08", "type": "concept", "title": "State and nonstate violence"}, {"id": "f5e3be89-37c9-491d-bff6-84dfb3508969", "type": "concept", "title": "Security forces and militias"}], "description": "Students compare forms of violence and their political logic."}, {"id": "7d6ad6a8-14ef-43b2-a9cd-0a13e9ea7c44", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Conflict causes", "children": [{"id": "448129cc-017d-4f6c-8e79-b371264d0caf", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethnic security dilemmas"}, {"id": "a2ab8d1f-cd43-4a87-8057-76b3237d4184", "type": "concept", "title": "Resource politics and grievance"}, {"id": "ab3f0773-aec4-49d2-a618-907fa2cc5e1e", "type": "concept", "title": "Weak states and external intervention"}], "description": "Conflict emerges from institutions, resources, identity, history, and opportunity."}, {"id": "4e10afdb-a738-4d95-817f-8a5d8018d792", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Post-conflict politics", "children": [{"id": "f68a0d0a-bbc6-4b1c-9d84-2bcf01ec16b8", "type": "concept", "title": "Peace agreements and power sharing"}, {"id": "73a71291-a258-43dc-a005-f8cdca748995", "type": "concept", "title": "Transitional justice"}, {"id": "d44d5d4e-3eaa-4e42-b7eb-703a62013ade", "type": "concept", "title": "Disarmament, demobilization, reintegration, and institution rebuilding"}], "description": "Ending violence is not the same as building durable peace."}]}, {"id": "1f1e8f44-d386-4abf-a4fd-838325eb04da", "type": "topic", "title": "Comparative Data, Case Studies, and Research Design", "description": "This module gives students the tools to compare countries rigorously using cases, datasets, and causal reasoning.", "children": [{"id": "25d8af50-1145-4450-95c1-258e52603c1c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative evidence", "children": [{"id": "cf9ae317-528e-4cea-bab9-7fca35161c88", "type": "concept", "title": "Country cases and small-N comparison"}, {"id": "f68920e4-bea9-496e-984d-81a532050c65", "type": "concept", "title": "Large-N datasets and cross-national indicators"}, {"id": "936e4e06-327d-43e4-b70d-40b810357863", "type": "concept", "title": "Triangulation and mixed evidence"}], "description": "Students learn to support claims with both qualitative and quantitative evidence."}, {"id": "26bb536b-8936-4cd2-bb1c-d226df17ae9c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Data sources", "children": [{"id": "e9c6059a-463f-44ae-9b0e-a15248dc997c", "type": "concept", "title": "V-Dem, Freedom House, World Bank, QoG, Our World in Data, and Comparative Constitutions Project"}, {"id": "85bf5511-d7b9-4d13-8618-081837feaeb8", "type": "concept", "title": "Codebooks and measurement choices"}, {"id": "44850d27-4a3d-465d-ad02-84475f477baa", "type": "concept", "title": "Missing data and comparability problems"}], "description": "World-class comparative politics uses transparent, credible data."}, {"id": "69ef68e7-f2eb-4390-8994-6844a456ce70", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research design", "children": [{"id": "78e47368-83f4-4507-818e-130e62a753af", "type": "concept", "title": "Hypotheses and mechanisms"}, {"id": "cb5faba8-fe9e-439b-bf50-69f13c528877", "type": "concept", "title": "Process tracing and counterfactuals"}, {"id": "2222d511-c4ca-43d0-8434-9c9dde10add8", "type": "concept", "title": "Confounding, endogeneity, and alternative explanations"}], "description": "Students learn basic causal reasoning for comparative arguments."}]}, {"id": "dc089e8a-bac3-41b8-951c-10d06ea1e282", "type": "topic", "title": "World Regions, Comparative Case Portfolio, and Capstone", "description": "Students synthesize the course by comparing real cases across regions and presenting a disciplined comparative argument.", "children": [{"id": "cddffab0-7cf5-41e1-9620-66e7bdbbad9c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regional comparison", "children": [{"id": "0ffb7f7e-0a2d-4cfd-bd67-3364a445ee8f", "type": "concept", "title": "Europe, Latin America, Africa, Middle East, South Asia, East Asia, and post-Soviet cases"}, {"id": "876538ca-5005-418c-8251-40b319e03396", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonialism, religion, development, conflict, and institutional legacies"}, {"id": "bad4fb7e-09c9-4cea-b5a8-ae6f389ec09f", "type": "concept", "title": "Regional variation and within-region comparison"}], "description": "Students learn enough regional context to avoid flattening countries into generic examples."}, {"id": "0ef805ef-e91e-4e46-b2e1-d0cc9bb553f6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Case portfolio", "children": [{"id": "d0db79b6-c248-4fc3-9ca8-80005c0aaa37", "type": "concept", "title": "Country profile with regime, institutions, economy, and identities"}, {"id": "d1cec7fe-5923-44f3-a0b2-b26c87cbe40f", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative memo using two or three countries"}, {"id": "6ca694c4-9a1a-41d8-9c3e-f6eeb24e44e4", "type": "concept", "title": "Data visualization and source annotation"}], "description": "A portfolio builds cumulative comparative judgment across the course."}, {"id": "66ce5509-4c20-4d0b-aa9b-65e2a2cd9885", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Capstone argument", "children": [{"id": "6d18cc03-9f58-4e76-922e-fbd2eb5629c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Puzzle, theory, evidence, and counterargument"}, {"id": "351cf8d7-abfa-4043-9f35-c1a8764c4c51", "type": "concept", "title": "Case selection rationale"}, {"id": "d28040b1-8df3-408c-8d1e-0ba401bef29d", "type": "concept", "title": "Policy or civic implication"}], "description": "Students synthesize concepts, evidence, and writing into a defensible comparative claim."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$cpoljson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  'course',
  'public'
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.learning_paths where slug = 'introduction-to-comparative-politics'
);

update public.learning_paths
set
  title = 'Introduction to Comparative Politics',
  goal = 'Introduction to Comparative Politics',
  summary = 'Compare political systems through field scope, the comparative method, concepts, measurement, and causal puzzles. Topics cover the state and state-building, nations and citizenship, democracy and democratic quality, authoritarian and hybrid regimes, constitutions and courts, parliamentary and presidential systems, electoral systems and parties, political culture and media, development and welfare states, political inclusion, protest and regime change, conflict and post-conflict governance, comparative data and research design, and a regional case portfolio.',
  data = $cpoljson${"slug": "introduction-to-comparative-politics", "title": "Introduction to Comparative Politics", "description": "Compare political systems through field scope, the comparative method, concepts, measurement, and causal puzzles. Topics cover the state and state-building, nations and citizenship, democracy and democratic quality, authoritarian and hybrid regimes, constitutions and courts, parliamentary and presidential systems, electoral systems and parties, political culture and media, development and welfare states, political inclusion, protest and regime change, conflict and post-conflict governance, comparative data and research design, and a regional case portfolio.", "topics": [{"id": "30a191d5-2b42-4160-a256-15bd059da8e9", "type": "topic", "title": "Comparative Politics as a Field and Method", "description": "Students learn why comparing political systems reveals patterns that single-country study can miss, while also learning the limits of comparison.", "children": [{"id": "dd535d52-db9a-4fdb-9766-0516886f4d21", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Field purpose", "children": [{"id": "68a85bd1-b924-4c42-ba78-cea7e3659309", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative politics versus American politics, IR, and political theory"}, {"id": "463e90ec-6164-4978-9538-3a8219f89e51", "type": "concept", "title": "Regimes, institutions, political behavior, and political economy"}, {"id": "f821bf60-f211-441e-8e6b-39f6f8292f2d", "type": "concept", "title": "Description, explanation, prediction, and normative evaluation"}], "description": "The point is to understand political variation across countries and explain why institutions, identities, and outcomes differ."}, {"id": "6c0089be-c52b-4c31-bf65-fb66e053f63d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative method", "children": [{"id": "095a6665-96c3-40a4-a577-1c28a8576025", "type": "concept", "title": "Most-similar and most-different case comparisons"}, {"id": "2b40e48b-6b0c-46f6-8090-a619a2d98c30", "type": "concept", "title": "Within-case and cross-national comparison"}, {"id": "67eaf761-70f9-447e-9ff6-d7726dbc42f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Selection bias and scope conditions"}], "description": "Students learn that comparison is a disciplined way of reasoning, not just lining countries up side by side."}, {"id": "c2be65a5-c83e-4049-a120-cce77fb82b33", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Concepts and measurement", "children": [{"id": "47570408-bca1-4625-9d7d-5f25b8211764", "type": "concept", "title": "Democracy, state capacity, development, identity, and violence"}, {"id": "2edebf45-3bdd-4cf0-8a3b-ff428466c0aa", "type": "concept", "title": "Operationalization and indicators"}, {"id": "5ec3f381-2867-4ead-ace2-c7160a8e6d44", "type": "concept", "title": "Validity, reliability, and contested concepts"}], "description": "Good comparison depends on defining concepts clearly and measuring them carefully."}]}, {"id": "725db581-1bd2-4761-aa7c-1c93efef9f1a", "type": "topic", "title": "The State, Sovereignty, Capacity, and State-Building", "description": "This module examines how states form, claim authority, build institutions, and deliver order, rights, and services.", "children": [{"id": "abc6ba38-1b9b-49d0-852a-58d3ead238ab", "type": "subtopic", "title": "State foundations", "children": [{"id": "3b49eafd-b3cd-4487-92b2-39addf182da6", "type": "concept", "title": "Territory, population, authority, and monopoly on legitimate violence"}, {"id": "92048feb-84d0-4ed4-9c6c-5e21e2dc2667", "type": "concept", "title": "Sovereignty and recognition"}, {"id": "758e3dac-7eaa-4e05-9621-6aad28f3de34", "type": "concept", "title": "State, regime, government, and nation distinctions"}], "description": "Students learn what makes a state distinct from a government, regime, nation, or society."}, {"id": "1e69214d-0760-417a-b03f-ca5d6cb98e55", "type": "subtopic", "title": "State-building", "children": [{"id": "5c790de8-539e-4d2c-90d0-236146a4fcb7", "type": "concept", "title": "War-making and extraction"}, {"id": "14448170-6633-4315-b3db-d7e539e651dd", "type": "concept", "title": "Bureaucracy and administrative capacity"}, {"id": "9cbde4b3-e111-4c91-9ec2-dc42697bc798", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial legacies and postcolonial state formation"}], "description": "State-building explains why some governments can tax, police, educate, and regulate while others struggle."}, {"id": "3796f552-a08d-4096-a795-5d86cd9e7882", "type": "subtopic", "title": "State capacity", "children": [{"id": "5411ea6f-78c0-41b0-aec5-d74e8e558517", "type": "concept", "title": "Taxation, public goods, rule implementation, and service delivery"}, {"id": "1791f8ba-9f5f-49c6-826a-476cdd6a9264", "type": "concept", "title": "Corruption, informality, and clientelism"}, {"id": "b9fc11e3-9d46-47d0-ab5d-07d04b146091", "type": "concept", "title": "Fragile states and governance gaps"}], "description": "Capacity links institutions to everyday outcomes people can feel."}]}, {"id": "4a2d2b96-6ea7-4e6b-96c6-b46ce30ff507", "type": "topic", "title": "Nations, Nationalism, Identity, and Citizenship", "description": "Students study how political communities are imagined, contested, included, and excluded through identity and citizenship.", "children": [{"id": "b3615fd8-253d-440e-bbf7-652eb9935b6c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Nation and nationalism", "children": [{"id": "51ae156d-d5e4-4828-b39c-94b8366391b0", "type": "concept", "title": "Civic and ethnic nationalism"}, {"id": "f9253b2d-c078-41c2-b5df-8c58322451b7", "type": "concept", "title": "Imagined communities and national myths"}, {"id": "28e182c8-4a7e-4277-81c9-03a388bf95e8", "type": "concept", "title": "National identity, borders, and belonging"}], "description": "Students learn how national identities become politically powerful and sometimes dangerous."}, {"id": "1fbf2b75-7005-4e65-ac8f-8d6f498e903a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Citizenship", "children": [{"id": "92a5f3f7-6ed4-4533-ade9-b2066c59197d", "type": "concept", "title": "Birthright, naturalization, and exclusion"}, {"id": "38dd5ba9-6384-46b4-b9c9-b253ffc868d4", "type": "concept", "title": "Migration, refugees, and statelessness"}, {"id": "2b6c6e73-7bab-44a9-99b4-e8902a4f79e6", "type": "concept", "title": "Citizenship rights and political participation"}], "description": "Citizenship defines who has rights, voice, obligations, and protection."}, {"id": "92ed6e3b-e64b-4790-be15-362b9ccf856a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Identity politics", "children": [{"id": "f2c73a38-ee89-418f-b38f-29438d32a495", "type": "concept", "title": "Language, ethnicity, religion, race, class, gender, and region"}, {"id": "6e2737d5-e41a-45a2-9b80-567415d67f8f", "type": "concept", "title": "Cross-cutting versus reinforcing cleavages"}, {"id": "ba6e7ab1-5fad-484f-939f-3fe08b01b271", "type": "concept", "title": "Identity entrepreneurs and political mobilization"}], "description": "Identity can mobilize solidarity, resistance, exclusion, and conflict."}]}, {"id": "1df364b7-24a0-4995-8d72-d31e3bd153ef", "type": "topic", "title": "Democracy, Representation, and Democratic Quality", "description": "This topic introduces democratic institutions, participation, rights, accountability, and the many ways democracies vary.", "children": [{"id": "f2bce05e-461b-4a75-8b54-f0df7a747090", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Democratic principles", "children": [{"id": "1f72d2f1-c19c-4bf2-947d-9484670a5773", "type": "concept", "title": "Competition and participation"}, {"id": "b1da4a4c-5c00-42e4-98d6-b0ae5fa8ad66", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil liberties and political rights"}, {"id": "797d231c-6129-4022-8895-886d94ff5788", "type": "concept", "title": "Rule of law, accountability, and consent"}], "description": "Students learn democracy as more than elections: it includes rights, accountability, and meaningful participation."}, {"id": "11804223-3477-4aa5-95a2-27a046639365", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Models of democracy", "children": [{"id": "71cf0865-c2d6-4f7a-9d45-ef24f87de4c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Majoritarian and consensus democracy"}, {"id": "2bdbeec0-1bab-4272-b8da-63ce40e4c861", "type": "concept", "title": "Direct, representative, deliberative, and participatory democracy"}, {"id": "ca53c309-1955-423f-9573-b3e212ad76bb", "type": "concept", "title": "Liberal, electoral, and social democracy"}], "description": "Different democracies distribute power and represent citizens in different ways."}, {"id": "3b6fd9c0-6c49-4b41-8b91-688710df14b6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Democratic quality", "children": [{"id": "4d35b0b9-288c-4e07-a5b6-c5fa7dc499e1", "type": "concept", "title": "V-Dem and Freedom House measures"}, {"id": "3e7311c2-6f97-4ad3-8a2a-2935cf9337ef", "type": "concept", "title": "Turnout, representation, inequality, corruption, and trust"}, {"id": "cc4cde12-241b-4571-91d0-152469b213d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Responsiveness and accountability"}], "description": "Students learn to evaluate democracies comparatively using evidence."}]}, {"id": "8d15284b-49a1-48d6-8da3-e7039d377a0a", "type": "topic", "title": "Authoritarian Regimes, Hybrid Regimes, and Democratic Backsliding", "description": "Students learn how nondemocratic rule works, why hybrid regimes persist, and how democratic erosion unfolds.", "children": [{"id": "92d675d2-360d-41bd-8d8e-798367b84f8a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Authoritarian types", "children": [{"id": "b345b953-5100-4384-89dd-6fee65e49de3", "type": "concept", "title": "Military, party, personalist, monarchic, and theocratic rule"}, {"id": "0fb15cf6-4f31-4d14-9124-56f065a0cb80", "type": "concept", "title": "Totalitarianism versus authoritarianism"}, {"id": "880fdb71-4296-4ab6-90e4-6a90acd803a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Selectorates, elites, coercion, and patronage"}], "description": "Students compare different nondemocratic systems rather than treating them as one category."}, {"id": "f060cb02-990d-45f8-be5f-9d735df84b45", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Hybrid regimes", "children": [{"id": "e37d6d0e-1000-4c0a-8031-1eb108e75f3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Competitive authoritarianism"}, {"id": "99ca8470-a120-4b4c-8311-eba4a0306f30", "type": "concept", "title": "Managed elections and controlled media"}, {"id": "298e247e-0379-4fcf-8896-073fe5ea6924", "type": "concept", "title": "Opposition constraints and institutional manipulation"}], "description": "Hybrid regimes use democratic-looking institutions without full democratic accountability."}, {"id": "0f5dc224-86e2-47ea-928e-d63e08b40966", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Backsliding", "children": [{"id": "d6e76586-f43b-49d7-9f91-bfbf3b62906d", "type": "concept", "title": "Court capture, media pressure, and electoral rule changes"}, {"id": "13c21b39-ee17-47fb-a5fd-977c9b5ef1d9", "type": "concept", "title": "Polarization and executive aggrandizement"}, {"id": "bcd0aaa4-a5dd-4d72-8a0e-e906e2aeabdd", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil society resistance and international pressure"}], "description": "Students learn how democracies can erode gradually through legal and institutional channels."}]}, {"id": "d18bb7ae-e18e-48ad-bfc7-1d70c8273255", "type": "topic", "title": "Constitutions, Executives, Legislatures, and Courts", "description": "This module compares institutional designs and how they structure power, bargaining, accountability, and rights.", "children": [{"id": "f3db2bc7-ce2d-4214-aa31-5d90a842095b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Constitutions", "children": [{"id": "2fbf8e7a-fc37-4b3c-8247-8f816ded9ccb", "type": "concept", "title": "Written and unwritten constitutions"}, {"id": "e5abda3e-20a4-4ea5-991b-3381a09e904a", "type": "concept", "title": "Rights catalogs and amendment rules"}, {"id": "965c1a28-7213-4000-99e6-0101d3261e11", "type": "concept", "title": "Constitutional endurance and enforcement"}], "description": "Constitutions set the formal rules for authority, rights, and institutional conflict."}, {"id": "8f07048c-89da-4389-b14d-9d5a8f48f177", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Executives and legislatures", "children": [{"id": "ac020762-8c39-45d7-bb9f-4103ae1f92cc", "type": "concept", "title": "Presidents, prime ministers, cabinets, and heads of state"}, {"id": "cf0a2a02-47ea-4665-881e-5363e3c4e0c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Unicameral and bicameral legislatures"}, {"id": "2872d98b-25fe-4cc7-819f-123265a2ca31", "type": "concept", "title": "Agenda control, confidence votes, and oversight"}], "description": "Students compare how systems organize lawmaking, oversight, and political bargaining."}, {"id": "dcd5a853-2cc3-4dac-981a-9457accbc9c2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Courts", "children": [{"id": "77cad11c-34b7-4337-98c7-1bed27e65a64", "type": "concept", "title": "Judicial review"}, {"id": "67dab922-ae58-45ea-98d1-ff87dc3779ee", "type": "concept", "title": "Judicial independence"}, {"id": "cd60d44b-8f6a-45d6-85bc-1391c34445c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Constitutional courts and legal mobilization"}], "description": "Courts can protect rights, constrain power, or become political battlegrounds."}]}, {"id": "4b3feab3-6384-41e1-b3f3-1ec2115619fe", "type": "topic", "title": "Parliamentary, Presidential, Federal, and Unitary Systems", "description": "Students compare major constitutional architectures and their consequences for governance and representation.", "children": [{"id": "ca979671-6dcb-4846-afd5-90baa3da7945", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Parliamentary and presidential systems", "children": [{"id": "aaf7dae1-325f-4532-816c-fdaad1d3f4d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Cabinet responsibility and confidence"}, {"id": "99975c36-e158-4aeb-aa88-9b869caebcf2", "type": "concept", "title": "Separation of powers"}, {"id": "dce90f6a-6ec0-47ec-905f-d618f71d63ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Coalition governments and divided government"}], "description": "Students learn how executive-legislative design shapes accountability and instability."}, {"id": "7afa9191-d9dd-4de5-bc23-8c3df1185614", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Federalism and decentralization", "children": [{"id": "aa9ec1b7-a0de-4af9-8a79-50fefc186fb0", "type": "concept", "title": "Federal, unitary, and devolved systems"}, {"id": "834c0b5a-3c1c-4998-9151-53cfd5ecb914", "type": "concept", "title": "Fiscal federalism"}, {"id": "85cb0087-a593-4adc-acc0-5b5bcdad23d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Subnational autonomy and minority accommodation"}], "description": "Territorial design affects identity, public goods, conflict, and policy variation."}, {"id": "24677b03-0afb-493f-bfbd-cee6722fdf7a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Institutional tradeoffs", "children": [{"id": "63da0bd5-ea03-4860-8877-43c8e5431e16", "type": "concept", "title": "Accountability versus inclusion"}, {"id": "7edcefa1-c114-4db1-b7bf-03d4f4c1343a", "type": "concept", "title": "Coordination versus veto points"}, {"id": "f8ea1060-8675-4bf7-98ba-9471317d8d25", "type": "concept", "title": "Stability versus responsiveness"}], "description": "No design is automatically best; institutions solve some problems and create others."}]}, {"id": "dd399bf5-9b07-496c-a9bb-a563099df05a", "type": "topic", "title": "Electoral Systems, Parties, Coalitions, and Voters", "description": "This topic connects electoral rules to party systems, campaigning, coalition formation, and voter behavior.", "children": [{"id": "93fbbe21-397e-46d0-bc31-d4b32c14ed9d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Electoral systems", "children": [{"id": "a741df34-71d7-43b4-83e4-6cb618a2cac2", "type": "concept", "title": "Single-member plurality, runoff, proportional representation, and mixed systems"}, {"id": "c6e060d1-a51f-4b64-bfbd-f632993d9df2", "type": "concept", "title": "District magnitude and thresholds"}, {"id": "85756872-0eea-49ab-9ac5-5b5f377cd650", "type": "concept", "title": "Duverger's law and mechanical/psychological effects"}], "description": "Rules translate votes into seats and shape strategic behavior."}, {"id": "6b098c11-19af-476f-b88a-1b18c479a3c6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Parties and party systems", "children": [{"id": "4317b837-3cb1-44af-91f9-2848f3f79c34", "type": "concept", "title": "Party institutionalization"}, {"id": "a47e6c69-8f5a-4e96-931e-8a0bb55eca1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Programmatic versus clientelist parties"}, {"id": "d6cbfc16-de6e-4055-8047-5ebe76da57d2", "type": "concept", "title": "Dominant-party, two-party, and multiparty systems"}], "description": "Parties organize representation, competition, policy, and governance."}, {"id": "3da67146-e35b-42c6-b777-63db27018897", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Voters and coalitions", "children": [{"id": "4fff1f05-7a34-4a9a-b187-085eab425b4b", "type": "concept", "title": "Cleavage voting, class voting, ethnic voting, and issue voting"}, {"id": "a1f2f2f2-282a-4e13-b248-6b7fdca24e31", "type": "concept", "title": "Campaigns, media, and turnout"}, {"id": "cbb41016-4970-478d-b0ca-fd4c9706fbcd", "type": "concept", "title": "Coalition bargaining and government formation"}], "description": "Students connect institutions to voter behavior and governing alliances."}]}, {"id": "4d3d5d2f-029e-4d2e-a54e-861ff13ff8b4", "type": "topic", "title": "Political Culture, Civil Society, Media, and Public Opinion", "description": "Students examine the social foundations of politics, including trust, participation, information, and civic organization.", "children": [{"id": "a51e4959-2909-4d6e-aca4-965bbac99e27", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Political culture", "children": [{"id": "e9972b37-3174-4237-86d8-bb62ccc8c2bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Trust, legitimacy, tolerance, and civic norms"}, {"id": "2a1ee3fc-c2a7-46f0-9a00-43aca1d2810c", "type": "concept", "title": "Political socialization"}, {"id": "bfce5dd7-ed43-444e-89d7-5199a123c598", "type": "concept", "title": "Culture as explanation and its limits"}], "description": "Political beliefs and norms influence how institutions are understood and used."}, {"id": "85897c1d-e245-4e43-a0ec-156731521ec7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Civil society", "children": [{"id": "1962de9d-49a2-4d1e-a513-9912caa05aee", "type": "concept", "title": "NGOs, unions, religious groups, professional groups, and social networks"}, {"id": "e82d72cd-1451-4b54-bb32-eeaf5e956470", "type": "concept", "title": "Social capital"}, {"id": "e67c8e30-6d3d-4c37-8bcd-a75a9ce85330", "type": "concept", "title": "State-civil society relations"}], "description": "Associations can check power, build trust, and mobilize citizens."}, {"id": "3fcf6fa2-1002-4785-8f8e-4fe4f042327a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Media and public opinion", "children": [{"id": "d70de851-0417-4904-b6b7-8b79c0c325c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Public opinion measurement"}, {"id": "c612b2da-d6d0-47fb-8f39-e75b45848b2c", "type": "concept", "title": "Media freedom and censorship"}, {"id": "9e6fd367-f743-4750-9c92-22a6cba04571", "type": "concept", "title": "Misinformation, propaganda, and platform politics"}], "description": "Information systems shape accountability, polarization, and political knowledge."}]}, {"id": "3b540eb3-441e-40ec-91f9-517f4c31ff0a", "type": "topic", "title": "Political Economy of Development, Inequality, and Welfare States", "description": "This module explains how institutions, markets, class, inequality, and policy shape development and redistribution.", "children": [{"id": "6368c700-cfb9-48cd-bbb3-4bda00951e74", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Development", "children": [{"id": "b1fe1eb3-cac0-4b2e-8682-e886bacc3a94", "type": "concept", "title": "Inclusive and extractive institutions"}, {"id": "98f8dda3-e307-42bf-a38c-38f8c88c5f81", "type": "concept", "title": "Modernization theory and dependency perspectives"}, {"id": "eeb8e439-9b30-438e-abf2-321dd673ca35", "type": "concept", "title": "Human development, poverty, and governance indicators"}], "description": "Students learn how political institutions shape economic growth and human development."}, {"id": "2035d47e-80d0-434f-b1f0-8ac7993be52b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Inequality and redistribution", "children": [{"id": "602c0a50-dcea-4380-b6ae-41c1caf23c93", "type": "concept", "title": "Class coalitions and labor politics"}, {"id": "cb7fcada-1147-44b6-84e0-deee3ca27e64", "type": "concept", "title": "Taxation, social insurance, and redistribution"}, {"id": "ad091aac-866b-4f3b-8325-0743fe076d29", "type": "concept", "title": "Inequality, mobility, and political voice"}], "description": "Politics determines who benefits from growth and who bears costs."}, {"id": "beb8213b-1333-489d-b36a-b7a1403dba44", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Welfare states and capitalism", "children": [{"id": "31fa6a86-04fe-43f5-aaf0-5daebd3f6832", "type": "concept", "title": "Liberal, conservative, social democratic, and developmental models"}, {"id": "c30b42ae-065a-454f-a14a-7d2b1935a243", "type": "concept", "title": "Varieties of capitalism"}, {"id": "1ff2c431-5a36-4a7a-a67a-23c3db946aae", "type": "concept", "title": "Austerity, globalization, and welfare reform"}], "description": "Countries organize markets and social protection in different ways."}]}, {"id": "203bde21-e281-40fb-9222-fefa1ed4e5d5", "type": "topic", "title": "Ethnicity, Religion, Gender, Race, and Political Inclusion", "description": "Students study how social cleavages become politically organized and how institutions can reduce or intensify exclusion.", "children": [{"id": "21fbd0a1-66a6-43a5-9af0-0483f9a56976", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Social cleavages", "children": [{"id": "e81543ee-e8ff-4b9b-892d-b1a11bebe883", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethnic, religious, racial, caste, class, gender, and regional cleavages"}, {"id": "2de12143-3b48-40af-8d9a-82de67d26507", "type": "concept", "title": "Cross-cutting and cumulative divisions"}, {"id": "f3e0f3ab-d832-4d0a-90ef-ca446b7006ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Cleavage freezing and transformation"}], "description": "Cleavages become politically important when identities align with organization, interests, and institutions."}, {"id": "4dd92414-6c27-446e-b949-6bdd3d3d485c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Inclusion and exclusion", "children": [{"id": "445689a9-9fe3-47a8-8cbb-34fb307c4055", "type": "concept", "title": "Minority rights and representation"}, {"id": "6bc48550-7ddd-44e2-b813-b20e17543a84", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender quotas and descriptive representation"}, {"id": "ee1519e9-2702-47b9-bf48-53e7ba8a7471", "type": "concept", "title": "Discrimination, segregation, and unequal citizenship"}], "description": "Political systems vary in how they include marginalized groups."}, {"id": "64478886-747b-4860-b08c-8eb50a44955e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Institutional management", "children": [{"id": "7fc6db70-be1f-4296-8003-e1dc8d9fb94f", "type": "concept", "title": "Consociationalism and power sharing"}, {"id": "a1a75086-a43c-4e0c-81ac-143acea1ef64", "type": "concept", "title": "Federalism and autonomy"}, {"id": "8c187d9b-33e7-46bb-886c-338c7fe82b0e", "type": "concept", "title": "Electoral incentives and identity moderation"}], "description": "Institutions can either lower conflict or harden divisions."}]}, {"id": "217735f9-5907-4dd6-8390-777f28db09e3", "type": "topic", "title": "Revolutions, Social Movements, Protest, and Regime Change", "description": "This topic covers collective action, contentious politics, revolutionary moments, repression, and transitions.", "children": [{"id": "1c36cd40-20f1-4510-9cd9-76880948f514", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Collective action", "children": [{"id": "1f16738c-46e2-42b4-8506-196b75e97df7", "type": "concept", "title": "Grievances, resources, and political opportunities"}, {"id": "f85edb1b-1e87-4bd2-b18a-462dfe933edd", "type": "concept", "title": "Networks, leadership, and framing"}, {"id": "62eba28d-2c5b-407f-bc39-3cec24e6cf23", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital mobilization and repression"}], "description": "Students learn why people mobilize despite risk and coordination problems."}, {"id": "d639e7ee-ac40-4475-b713-0bd234d7c40f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Revolutions and transitions", "children": [{"id": "60616ac1-eadc-465e-9fea-e7af87062c98", "type": "concept", "title": "Social revolutions"}, {"id": "04026b76-e5fa-456a-935d-7250970449c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Negotiated transitions"}, {"id": "e0e69d63-969d-4d7f-ad30-9019dd798655", "type": "concept", "title": "Coups and pacts"}], "description": "Regime change is shaped by elite splits, mass mobilization, and institutional breakdown."}, {"id": "9bb150ba-b926-486e-9a9e-2cba940b6105", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Protest outcomes", "children": [{"id": "a39ffcd0-827f-4f83-ac5f-e9d8b9b0e016", "type": "concept", "title": "Nonviolent versus violent resistance"}, {"id": "9e7c4a57-a287-4013-ba14-833c4a716d45", "type": "concept", "title": "Repression and backlash"}, {"id": "6126f997-4fbe-4ef3-9ac9-0f272d1f8576", "type": "concept", "title": "Movement institutionalization and policy change"}], "description": "Movements can democratize, radicalize, be absorbed, or be repressed."}]}, {"id": "44d06b12-5789-4918-946c-b7b4fdaf151f", "type": "topic", "title": "Conflict, Violence, Nationalism, and Post-Conflict Governance", "description": "Students analyze civil conflict, state violence, nationalism, peacebuilding, and post-conflict institutions.", "children": [{"id": "f11e3c6e-8df8-47bd-89aa-2a19417a4936", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Political violence", "children": [{"id": "5ccda3ad-2775-438c-88d2-4c5a721b9ab0", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil war, terrorism, repression, riots, and insurgency"}, {"id": "cf862d56-2ef3-4aa1-aded-ac72a4203d08", "type": "concept", "title": "State and nonstate violence"}, {"id": "f5e3be89-37c9-491d-bff6-84dfb3508969", "type": "concept", "title": "Security forces and militias"}], "description": "Students compare forms of violence and their political logic."}, {"id": "7d6ad6a8-14ef-43b2-a9cd-0a13e9ea7c44", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Conflict causes", "children": [{"id": "448129cc-017d-4f6c-8e79-b371264d0caf", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethnic security dilemmas"}, {"id": "a2ab8d1f-cd43-4a87-8057-76b3237d4184", "type": "concept", "title": "Resource politics and grievance"}, {"id": "ab3f0773-aec4-49d2-a618-907fa2cc5e1e", "type": "concept", "title": "Weak states and external intervention"}], "description": "Conflict emerges from institutions, resources, identity, history, and opportunity."}, {"id": "4e10afdb-a738-4d95-817f-8a5d8018d792", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Post-conflict politics", "children": [{"id": "f68a0d0a-bbc6-4b1c-9d84-2bcf01ec16b8", "type": "concept", "title": "Peace agreements and power sharing"}, {"id": "73a71291-a258-43dc-a005-f8cdca748995", "type": "concept", "title": "Transitional justice"}, {"id": "d44d5d4e-3eaa-4e42-b7eb-703a62013ade", "type": "concept", "title": "Disarmament, demobilization, reintegration, and institution rebuilding"}], "description": "Ending violence is not the same as building durable peace."}]}, {"id": "1f1e8f44-d386-4abf-a4fd-838325eb04da", "type": "topic", "title": "Comparative Data, Case Studies, and Research Design", "description": "This module gives students the tools to compare countries rigorously using cases, datasets, and causal reasoning.", "children": [{"id": "25d8af50-1145-4450-95c1-258e52603c1c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative evidence", "children": [{"id": "cf9ae317-528e-4cea-bab9-7fca35161c88", "type": "concept", "title": "Country cases and small-N comparison"}, {"id": "f68920e4-bea9-496e-984d-81a532050c65", "type": "concept", "title": "Large-N datasets and cross-national indicators"}, {"id": "936e4e06-327d-43e4-b70d-40b810357863", "type": "concept", "title": "Triangulation and mixed evidence"}], "description": "Students learn to support claims with both qualitative and quantitative evidence."}, {"id": "26bb536b-8936-4cd2-bb1c-d226df17ae9c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Data sources", "children": [{"id": "e9c6059a-463f-44ae-9b0e-a15248dc997c", "type": "concept", "title": "V-Dem, Freedom House, World Bank, QoG, Our World in Data, and Comparative Constitutions Project"}, {"id": "85bf5511-d7b9-4d13-8618-081837feaeb8", "type": "concept", "title": "Codebooks and measurement choices"}, {"id": "44850d27-4a3d-465d-ad02-84475f477baa", "type": "concept", "title": "Missing data and comparability problems"}], "description": "World-class comparative politics uses transparent, credible data."}, {"id": "69ef68e7-f2eb-4390-8994-6844a456ce70", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research design", "children": [{"id": "78e47368-83f4-4507-818e-130e62a753af", "type": "concept", "title": "Hypotheses and mechanisms"}, {"id": "cb5faba8-fe9e-439b-bf50-69f13c528877", "type": "concept", "title": "Process tracing and counterfactuals"}, {"id": "2222d511-c4ca-43d0-8434-9c9dde10add8", "type": "concept", "title": "Confounding, endogeneity, and alternative explanations"}], "description": "Students learn basic causal reasoning for comparative arguments."}]}, {"id": "dc089e8a-bac3-41b8-951c-10d06ea1e282", "type": "topic", "title": "World Regions, Comparative Case Portfolio, and Capstone", "description": "Students synthesize the course by comparing real cases across regions and presenting a disciplined comparative argument.", "children": [{"id": "cddffab0-7cf5-41e1-9620-66e7bdbbad9c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regional comparison", "children": [{"id": "0ffb7f7e-0a2d-4cfd-bd67-3364a445ee8f", "type": "concept", "title": "Europe, Latin America, Africa, Middle East, South Asia, East Asia, and post-Soviet cases"}, {"id": "876538ca-5005-418c-8251-40b319e03396", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonialism, religion, development, conflict, and institutional legacies"}, {"id": "bad4fb7e-09c9-4cea-b5a8-ae6f389ec09f", "type": "concept", "title": "Regional variation and within-region comparison"}], "description": "Students learn enough regional context to avoid flattening countries into generic examples."}, {"id": "0ef805ef-e91e-4e46-b2e1-d0cc9bb553f6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Case portfolio", "children": [{"id": "d0db79b6-c248-4fc3-9ca8-80005c0aaa37", "type": "concept", "title": "Country profile with regime, institutions, economy, and identities"}, {"id": "d1cec7fe-5923-44f3-a0b2-b26c87cbe40f", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative memo using two or three countries"}, {"id": "6ca694c4-9a1a-41d8-9c3e-f6eeb24e44e4", "type": "concept", "title": "Data visualization and source annotation"}], "description": "A portfolio builds cumulative comparative judgment across the course."}, {"id": "66ce5509-4c20-4d0b-aa9b-65e2a2cd9885", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Capstone argument", "children": [{"id": "6d18cc03-9f58-4e76-922e-fbd2eb5629c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Puzzle, theory, evidence, and counterargument"}, {"id": "351cf8d7-abfa-4043-9f35-c1a8764c4c51", "type": "concept", "title": "Case selection rationale"}, {"id": "d28040b1-8df3-408c-8d1e-0ba401bef29d", "type": "concept", "title": "Policy or civic implication"}], "description": "Students synthesize concepts, evidence, and writing into a defensible comparative claim."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$cpoljson$::jsonb,
